## Report: Infrastructure failure — no code changes needed

**Conclusion:** This is a transient CI/runner infrastructure issue, not a code regression caused by any commit on this branch.

### Root cause

The job `efi-x86_64-uboot-iso` failed in `scripts/ubuntu-iso-to-uboot.py` while preparing the test ISO:

```
File ".../scripts/ubuntu-iso-to-uboot.py", line 197, in repack_iso
    out_iso.unlink()
PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted: '/tmp/ubuntu-24.04.1-uboot.iso'
```

The script tries to remove a stale output file (`/tmp/ubuntu-24.04.1-uboot.iso`) before xorriso writes a fresh one. The unlink fails with `EPERM` — the file is left over from a previous run on the `ellesmere-lab` runner and is owned by a different user/process, so the current runner user cannot delete it. This is a `/tmp` ownership/cleanup issue on the shared runner, not a U-Boot bug.

The downstream "Lab failure: Marking connection bad" message is just labgrid reporting that the bootstrap step failed — caused entirely by the unlink error above.

### Why no commit on this branch is responsible

The branch (12 commits) modifies only Linux-compat headers, ofnode helpers, vsprintf, regmap, etc. — see `git diff ci/master..HEAD --stat`:

- `Kconfig`, `common/board_f.c`, `drivers/core/{of_access,ofnode}.c`,
- `include/{asm-generic/sections.h,dm/of_access.h,dm/ofnode.h,fdtdec.h,linux/*,power/regulator.h,regmap.h}`,
- `lib/{fdtdec,string,vsprintf}.c`, plus `.pickman-history`.

None of these commits touch:
- `scripts/ubuntu-iso-to-uboot.py`
- `test/py/tests/test_distro.py`
- any EFI ISO test infrastructure
- anything related to `/tmp` file management

`git log ci/master..HEAD -- scripts/ubuntu-iso-to-uboot.py test/py/tests/test_distro.py` returns empty.

### Recommended action

Re-run the job after cleaning up `/tmp/ubuntu-24.04.1-uboot.iso` on the `ellesmere-lab` runner (or fix its ownership). A long-term fix would be to make the script tolerant of `EPERM` on unlink (e.g., write to a per-job/per-user temporary path under `/tmp/<runner-id>/` instead of a fixed shared name), but that is outside the scope of this MR.

