- Check make qcheck (test.py)

**Root cause for "build all 32bit ARM platforms" — actually caused by this MR:**

The new r8a78000_ironhide_cm33 board (added in commit 825afe28924
"arm: renesas: Add Renesas R-Car R8A78000 X5H Cortex-M33 RSIP port")
fails the world build with:

  u-boot.map shows a binary size of -406913023
    but u-boot-nodtb.bin shows 460328
  make[1]: *** [Makefile:1623: binary_size_check] Error 1

Reproduced locally with `um build r8a78000_ironhide_cm33`. The
previous attempt's diagnosis ("unrelated 32-bit ARM linker quirk")
was wrong — this is the new aarch32 (Cortex-M33) target introduced
by these cherry-picks.

Looking at u-boot.map for the failing build:

  0x18410000  __image_copy_start = ADDR (.text)
  0x18480628  _image_binary_end = .
  0x00000001  ASSERT (((_image_binary_end % 0x8) == 0x0), ...)

The Makefile's `binary_size_check` rule uses awk patterns
`/_image_copy_start/` and `/_image_binary_end/` which match
substrings. The new u-boot-rsip.lds is the first main u-boot.lds
(not SPL) to have an ASSERT referencing `_image_binary_end`. The
ASSERT line ends up in the map with value 0x1 (its return value),
overwriting the real `_image_binary_end = 0x18480628` capture, so
`end - start = 1 - 0x18410000 = -406913023`.

**Fix:** Dropped the redundant `ASSERT(_image_binary_end % 8 == 0, ...)`
from `arch/arm/mach-renesas/u-boot-rsip.lds`. The alignment it
guards is already guaranteed by `. = ALIGN(8)` at the close of the
`.rel.dyn` section (the section itself is `ALIGN(8)` aligned, and
the explicit internal align makes the section end 8-aligned, which
is where `_image_binary_end = .` is captured). No other main
u-boot.lds in tree references `_image_binary_end` from an ASSERT.

